# What `--emit-c` owes, and the two clang questions it must ask before it
# writes a byte (defect 048, panel 157, 2026-09-16).
#
# Split out of `cli/produce.hero` the day the work landed, when that file
# passed §11's 300 lines. **The seam is a concern and not a line count**:
# `produce` answers *how a program becomes a binary* and this answers *what the
# artifact of a stopped build owes*, which is a different question with a
# different verdict — `produce` may refuse a program, and this may only refuse
# the ARTIFACT.
#
# ## The defect this file is the repair for
#
# The write used to live in `cli/compile.hero`, which returned before
# `cli/produce.hero` ran at all. So the artifact was the FIRST emission: before
# the pointee check, which is a refusal, and before the tag round, which learns
# a spelling from clang's own words. `--emit-c` owes **the artifact of a build
# that PASSED**, and *C that clang accepts* is the weaker bar — five
# `tests/golden/fixedbugs/` cases satisfied it while writing over the stack.
#
# ## Two questions, two shapes, and they are opposites
#
# **The pointee check REFUSES.** A binding declaring `@n: i32` against a header's
# `uint64_t *` emitted at exit 0, compiled clean under the real flag list, and
# wrote `0xAAAAAAAA` over the next stack slot — design.md §1.12, measured. Its
# *no* is the whole point, so it must be able to say it.
#
# **The tag probe NEVER refuses.** It asks clang the one question the build's
# round asks — does this header want `struct` in front of the author's word —
# and throws the verdict away, keeping only the spelling. That is what makes it
# safe here: fourteen `fixedbugs/` bindings exist precisely to have C where a
# build has none, and a refusal would delete them. A probe that can only ADD a
# spelling cannot turn an emission into a failure, and on a machine without the
# header it learns nothing and the output is yesterday's.
#
# Treating the two alike would have broken whichever one it was not written for.
#
# ## Why clang and not a rule
#
# Four header shapes, compiled at the sitting: `struct X *` fails on a typedef
# of an ANONYMOUS struct, the bare word fails on a tag-only header, and a
# typedef shim at the top of the artifact fails on the anonymous shape too. No
# fixed spelling is correct. The only clang-free route left is a C declaration
# parser inside Heroes, which the compiler seat's veto is aimed at.
#
# ## The one-way edge
#
# This file does the work and returns a verdict; `cli/produce.hero` decides
# whose fault a failure is, because `blamed` lives there and is the round's too.
# Heroes refuses module cycles, so the arrow pointing one way is the condition
# under which this seam exists at all.

use source
use cli/io
use cli/compile
use cli/toolchain
use cli/pointee
use cli/pointee_ask

# The artifact, written or printed, after both questions have been asked. A
# failure here is the pointee check's and carries its message up so the caller
# can decide whose it is — this file never blames anybody.
function write_c(t: toolchain.Toolchain, options: compile.Options, art: compile.Artifact, s: source.Source, @answered: [pointee_ask.Answered], source_dir: str, package_flags: [str]) -> ()?
    checked = pointee.check(
        t,
        art.program,
        art.checked,
        s,
        art.decls,
        answered: @answered,
        search_include: options.headers_from,
        package_flags: package_flags,
        source_dir: source_dir
    )
    checked?

    # **THE TAG PROBE WAS HERE FOR ONE COMMIT AND IS WITHDRAWN, 2026-09-16.** It
    # asked clang whether a handle's tag needs `struct` in front, discarded the
    # verdict and re-emitted — and it worked: `structtag`'s artifact went from
    # 20 clang errors to 0. What it also did is make this artifact **a function
    # of the local headers**, and `emission` blesses these bytes. CI measured
    # the consequence within the hour: macOS learned `struct addrinfo` and
    # Windows, which has no `netdb.h`, learned nothing, so the same program
    # emitted two different files and the suite compared one against the other.
    #
    # That is defect 047's own class — a golden encoding one platform's
    # answer — committed inside the repair, on the day the rule naming it was
    # written. **A blessed byte-for-byte artifact cannot depend on the machine
    # it was produced on**, so either this probe goes or those programs stop
    # being blessed by bytes. The second is panel 157's R4, adopted and not
    # built, and the choice between them belongs to a sitting rather than to
    # the hour after a red leg.
    #
    # What stays is the half that outranks it: the pointee check above, which
    # refuses an artifact whose binding writes past its slot (§1.12). That one
    # cannot make the output platform-dependent, because it does not change a
    # byte — it decides whether there is an artifact at all.
    text: str @ art.c

    if options.output.is_err()
        io.print_artifact(text: text)
        return ok()
    written = write_file(path: options.output.must(), text: text)

    if written.is_err()
        io.eprint(text: "error: cannot write " + options.output.must())
        return fail(code: "failed", msg: "cannot write the artifact")

    return ok()
